  "query_preview": "..\nUpper level ridge of High pressure is still in control. This\nwill maintain the dry weather theme in spite of periodic \ncloudiness. Low level easterly winds will transport shallow\nlayer of moisture into the area from the east today...\nlingering tonight before waning Thursday. A transient area of\nupper level clouds (cirrus) will move from west to east across\nthe region today, exiting the region tonight. This upper level\ncloud element is the remnants of the weather system that brought\nflash flooding to the southern Rockies this past weekend...the\nsystem now reduced to a band of cirrus. \n\nAll of this extra cloud cover will likely keep temperatures \nmuted today...but still close to the seasonal normals. The \nwarmest day of the week is expected to be Friday as winds turn \nwesterly, skies clearing and giving a boost to the numbers.\nModel Output Statistics (MOS) indicate highs a full 10 degrees \nabove normal for Friday which means 70s for most folks and \naround 80 degrees in the Piedmont. \n\nAs for the next chance of rain. Models have been consistent \nwith advertisement of a southern stream short wave to cross the \ncountry over the weekend, bringing moisture into the area \nbeginning Sunday November 1st. Some rain may sneak in here late \nHalloween night, but all models still insist on dry conditions \nfor the Trick-or-Treat crowd Saturday evening.\n\nStrength of the initial wave Sunday appears weak, but models do\nindicate southeast upslope flow which should enhance the rain \nchance along the Blue Ridge. A series of disturbances are \nforecast to follow during the week, so it looks promising for \nadditional rainfall, and this should help snuff the late \nOctober dry spell.",
